module

public import LeanCategories.Lattices.Valued.WeylChambers

/-!
# The `(−2)`/`(−4)` rule for roots of an even 2-elementary lattice (`#116`)

AEGS, **printed p. 10**: *"The lattices `L^±_En` and `L^±_dP` are even 2-elementary. For such
lattices the roots are the `(−2)`-vectors and the `(−4)`-vectors with `div(α) = 2`."* The same page
gives the criterion the argument turns on: a root `α` satisfies `w_α(Λ) = Λ`
*"equivalently such that
`2 div(α) ∈ (α · α)ℤ`"*, i.e. `α² ∣ 2 div(α)`.

This file proves the forward direction of that rule, which is the half the folding chamber needs: in
an even lattice on which `div` divides `2`, every root has square `(−2)`, or square `(−4)` with all
pairings even. That is exactly the hypothesis `hsq` of
`isChamberWithWalls_invariant_of_square_criterion` (`FoldingChamberOfSquareCriterion.lean:53`),
which
the report had carried as Namikawa's classification (**printed p. 12**).

**Scope of the hypothesis, corrected — and a vacuity that this correction removes.** `h2e` is
`div(x) ∣ 2`, recorded as: `2` lies in the ideal generated by the values `(x · y)`. Two things about
it are worth stating, because an earlier version of this file got the second wrong.

* It is a hypothesis about *vectors*, and it must exclude `0`. Quantified over all `x` it is
  unsatisfiable — at `x = 0` every value `(0 · y)` vanishes, so no `ℤ`-combination of them is `2`
  (`twoElementary_hypothesis_fails_at_zero`, below). Stated that way the theorem was **vacuously
  true**, and nothing in the folding development consumed it, so no downstream statement was
affected;
  the theorems below now ask for `x ≠ 0`, which every root of negative square satisfies.
* Its exact strength relative to "even 2-elementary" is not settled here. What the source's rule
uses
  is `div ∣ 2` *at the root*, and the root criterion on **printed p. 10** gives `q ∈ {−g, −2g}` with
  `g = div(α)`; the rule follows from `g ∈ {1, 2}` there. This file takes `g ∣ 2` as data and does
  not derive it from 2-elementarity.

The argument is the source's, in three divisibility steps. Let `q = (α·α)`.

* `div(α) ∣ 2` is the hypothesis `h2e`, carried as data. It is recorded in the form the argument
  uses: `2` lies in the ideal generated by the values `(α · y)`, so there are `y₁, y₂, c₁, c₂` with
  `c₁ (α · y₁) + c₂ (α · y₂) = 2`. For integral lattices the ideal generated by `{(α · y)}` is
  principal, so this is `div(α) ∣ 2` and nothing more.
* `q ∣ 2 (α · y)` for every `y` is the reflective condition (`LinearMap.IsReflective.dvd_two_mul`).
  Applying it to `y₁, y₂` and multiplying the relation above by `2` gives `q ∣ 4`.
* `q < 0` and `q` even (the lattice is even) then leave `q = −2` or `q = −4`; and at `q = −4` the
  same reflective condition reads `−4 ∣ 2 (α · y)`, that is `2 ∣ (α · y)`, which is the second
  clause. Nothing here is a classification of root orbits.

* `pairing_eq_neg_two_or_neg_four_of_two_elementary` — one root;
* `square_criterion_of_two_elementary` — the same over `negativeReflectiveRoots L`, i.e. the shape
  the chamber theorems consume as `hsq`.

What this does **not** do: it does not show that `T_dP`, `T_En` or a folded fixed lattice `M^σ` is
even 2-elementary. That is the source's assertion on **p. 10**, cited and not reproved; the two
hypotheses are carried. It also does not give the converse direction of the rule (that every `(−2)`
vector, and every `(−4)` vector with `div = 2`, is a root), which is `IsReflective.of_dvd_two`.

## LC-09 record

Repository: `rg "two_elementary|TwoElementary"` gives only `ReducedDiscriminantData` (invariant
factors of a presentation, unrelated). Mathlib: `LinearMap.IsReflective` occurs only in
`LinearAlgebra/RootSystem/OfBilinear.lean`, which has no divisibility bound on the square.
Formalization corpus (`scripts/formalization_corpus.py`): `"two-elementary"` returns
`TauCeti/LinearAlgebra/IntegralLattice/TwoElementary.lean` and
`TauCeti/LinearAlgebra/FiniteBilinearModule/Dyadic/TwoElementary.lean`; both classify *discriminant
forms* (Gauss sums, Nikulin generators) for TauCeti's own finite quadratic module framework and
state
nothing about root squares in a bundled lattice, so neither supplies this statement and nothing is
ported. `"IsReflective sublattice"` and `"reflectiveRoot invariant"` return nothing.

## References

* V. Alexeev, P. Engel, D. Z. Garza, L. Schaffler, *Compact moduli of Enriques surfaces of degree
  2*,
  Nagoya Math. J. 259 (2025), arXiv:2312.03638v2: the root criterion `2 div(α) ∈ (α·α)ℤ`, the
  even 2-elementary rule and the `(−2)`/`(−4)` colouring, **printed p. 10**; the use of the rule as
  Lemma 3.4's converse, **printed p. 12**.
* V. V. Nikulin, *Integral symmetric bilinear forms and some of their applications*, §2
  (divisibility
  and the discriminant group), cited by the source at **printed p. 12** as [Nam85, 2.13 and 2.15].
-/

@[expose] public section

open LeanCategories.Modules.Bilinear.Valued

namespace LeanCategories.Lattices.Valued

variable {L : FiniteProjectiveLatticeCat ℤ ℤ}

/-- **The hypothesis `h2e` must exclude `0`.** Stated for every vector it is unsatisfiable: at
`x = 0` all values vanish, so no `ℤ`-combination of two of them can be `2`. A previous version of
the
rule below quantified over all `x` and was therefore vacuous. -/
theorem twoElementary_hypothesis_fails_at_zero :
    ¬ ∀ x : L.obj.obj.carrier, ∃ (y₁ y₂ : L.obj.obj.carrier) (c₁ c₂ : ℤ),
      c₁ * L.obj.obj.pairing x y₁ + c₂ * L.obj.obj.pairing x y₂ = 2 := by
  intro h
  obtain ⟨y₁, y₂, c₁, c₂, hc⟩ := h 0
  simp at hc

/-- **AEGS p. 10, forward direction.** In an even lattice on which `div` divides `2`, a root of
negative square has square `(−2)`, or `(−4)` with every pairing even.

`h2e` records `div(x) ∣ 2` in the form the argument uses: `2` in the ideal generated by the values
`(x · y)`, which for an integral lattice is the same condition. It is **not** implied by
2-elementarity — see `TwoElementaryDivisibilityScope.lean` — so it is carried, not discharged. -/
theorem pairing_eq_neg_two_or_neg_four_of_div_at
    (hev : ∀ y : L.obj.obj.carrier, (2 : ℤ) ∣ L.obj.obj.pairing y y)
    {x : L.obj.obj.carrier}
    (hdiv : ∃ (y₁ y₂ : L.obj.obj.carrier) (c₁ c₂ : ℤ),
      c₁ * L.obj.obj.pairing x y₁ + c₂ * L.obj.obj.pairing x y₂ = 2)
    (hx : IsReflectiveRoot L.obj x)
    (hneg : L.obj.obj.pairing x x < 0) :
    L.obj.obj.pairing x x = -2 ∨
      (L.obj.obj.pairing x x = -4 ∧ ∀ y : L.obj.obj.carrier, (2 : ℤ) ∣ L.obj.obj.pairing x y) := by
  obtain ⟨y₁, y₂, c₁, c₂, hcomb⟩ := hdiv
  have hdvd : ∀ y : L.obj.obj.carrier,
      L.obj.obj.pairing x x ∣ 2 * L.obj.obj.pairing x y := fun y => hx.dvd_two_mul y
  obtain ⟨a, ha⟩ := hdvd y₁
  obtain ⟨b, hb⟩ := hdvd y₂
  have hq4 : L.obj.obj.pairing x x ∣ 4 := by
    refine ⟨a * c₁ + b * c₂, ?_⟩
    calc (4 : ℤ)
        = 2 * (c₁ * L.obj.obj.pairing x y₁ + c₂ * L.obj.obj.pairing x y₂) := by rw [hcomb]; ring
      _ = c₁ * (2 * L.obj.obj.pairing x y₁) + c₂ * (2 * L.obj.obj.pairing x y₂) := by ring
      _ = c₁ * (L.obj.obj.pairing x x * a) + c₂ * (L.obj.obj.pairing x x * b) := by rw [ha, hb]
      _ = L.obj.obj.pairing x x * (a * c₁ + b * c₂) := by ring
  have hq_ne : L.obj.obj.pairing x x ≠ 0 := by linarith
  have hnat : (L.obj.obj.pairing x x).natAbs ∣ 4 := Int.natAbs_dvd_natAbs.mpr hq4
  have hle : (L.obj.obj.pairing x x).natAbs ≤ 4 := Nat.le_of_dvd (by norm_num) hnat
  have hq_eq : L.obj.obj.pairing x x = -(((L.obj.obj.pairing x x).natAbs : ℤ)) := by
    have h : ((L.obj.obj.pairing x x).natAbs : ℤ) = -L.obj.obj.pairing x x :=
      Int.ofNat_natAbs_of_nonpos (by linarith)
    omega
  obtain ⟨k, hk⟩ := hev x
  have hcases : L.obj.obj.pairing x x = -2 ∨ L.obj.obj.pairing x x = -4 := by omega
  rcases hcases with h | h
  · exact Or.inl h
  · refine Or.inr ⟨h, fun y => ?_⟩
    have hy := hdvd y
    rw [h] at hy
    obtain ⟨t, ht⟩ := hy
    exact ⟨-t, by linarith⟩

/-- **The rule, in the global form**: `div(x) ∣ 2` for every nonzero `x`. -/
theorem pairing_eq_neg_two_or_neg_four_of_two_elementary
    (hev : ∀ y : L.obj.obj.carrier, (2 : ℤ) ∣ L.obj.obj.pairing y y)
    (h2e : ∀ x : L.obj.obj.carrier, x ≠ 0 → ∃ (y₁ y₂ : L.obj.obj.carrier) (c₁ c₂ : ℤ),
      c₁ * L.obj.obj.pairing x y₁ + c₂ * L.obj.obj.pairing x y₂ = 2)
    {x : L.obj.obj.carrier} (hx : IsReflectiveRoot L.obj x)
    (hneg : L.obj.obj.pairing x x < 0) :
    L.obj.obj.pairing x x = -2 ∨
      (L.obj.obj.pairing x x = -4 ∧ ∀ y : L.obj.obj.carrier, (2 : ℤ) ∣ L.obj.obj.pairing x y) := by
  have hx0 : x ≠ 0 := by
    rintro rfl
    simp at hneg
  exact pairing_eq_neg_two_or_neg_four_of_div_at hev (h2e x hx0) hx hneg

/-- **The `(−2)`/`(−4)` rule over the negative reflective roots**, which is the hypothesis the
chamber theorems take as `hsq` (`FoldingChamberOfSquareCriterion.lean:53`). -/
theorem square_criterion_of_two_elementary
    (hev : ∀ y : L.obj.obj.carrier, (2 : ℤ) ∣ L.obj.obj.pairing y y)
    (h2e : ∀ x : L.obj.obj.carrier, x ≠ 0 → ∃ (y₁ y₂ : L.obj.obj.carrier) (c₁ c₂ : ℤ),
      c₁ * L.obj.obj.pairing x y₁ + c₂ * L.obj.obj.pairing x y₂ = 2) :
    ∀ ρ ∈ negativeReflectiveRoots L,
      L.obj.obj.pairing ρ ρ = -2 ∨
        (L.obj.obj.pairing ρ ρ = -4 ∧
          ∀ y : L.obj.obj.carrier, (2 : ℤ) ∣ L.obj.obj.pairing ρ y) :=
  fun ρ hρ => pairing_eq_neg_two_or_neg_four_of_two_elementary (x := ρ) hev h2e hρ.1 hρ.2

/-- **At a `(−2)`-vector the divisibility hypothesis is free**: `2 = −(x·x)`, so the single witness
`y₁ = y₂ = x` with `c₁ = −1`, `c₂ = 0` already gives `2`. The whole content of `div ∣ 2` therefore
sits at square `(−4)`. -/
theorem div_two_of_square_neg_two {x : L.obj.obj.carrier}
    (h : L.obj.obj.pairing x x = -2) :
    ∃ (y₁ y₂ : L.obj.obj.carrier) (c₁ c₂ : ℤ),
      c₁ * L.obj.obj.pairing x y₁ + c₂ * L.obj.obj.pairing x y₂ = 2 :=
  ⟨x, x, -1, 0, by rw [h]; ring⟩

end LeanCategories.Lattices.Valued
